WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.169 --> 00:00:07.169
Hello and welcome to this course, Interactive Materials for Gameplay.

2
00:00:07.169 --> 00:00:13.130
This course is based in Unreal Engine 5.5.

3
00:00:13.130 --> 00:00:23.129
Now, with this course comes some project files and we're going to be relying relatively heavily on the project for the course.

4
00:00:23.129 --> 00:00:32.450
It's going to have a number of different exercises and also a few different examples that you can pick apart in your own time.

5
00:00:32.450 --> 00:00:37.450
However, what we do use is something that's called the teaching aid for this.

6
00:00:37.450 --> 00:00:42.450
Now, hopefully this should fire up when you first log into the project.

7
00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:50.450
If it doesn't, you'll be able to find the teaching aid in the directory Content Teaching Tools Teaching Aid.

8
00:00:50.450 --> 00:01:00.700
It is a editor utility widget, so if you right-hand click on it, you'll have the choice to run editor utility widget.

9
00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:03.829
Now, I'll just demonstrate this quickly.

10
00:01:03.829 --> 00:01:11.310
So as you can see in the top right corner here, I have a forward, back, beginning and end button.

11
00:01:11.310 --> 00:01:16.310
And you'll see, as I flick through these, it will do a couple of things.

12
00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:22.310
It will move the editor camera around the level so that you're looking at the appropriate thing.

13
00:01:22.310 --> 00:01:31.310
And it will also change the content browser so we will be able to view the exact assets and examples that we need.

14
00:01:31.310 --> 00:01:36.310
So it just makes the whole thing a little bit easier to follow along with.

15
00:01:36.310 --> 00:01:42.310
And again, if you want to get hold of this and it doesn't automatically fire up,

16
00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:50.819
it does. You might want to just dock it in the top right hand corner here so it doesn't get in your way.

17
00:01:50.819 --> 00:02:00.920
You can grab it from training tools. You'll see there is a training aid here. Right-hand click and just push run editor utility widget.

18
00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:03.920
So as this course, we have a couple of goals.

19
00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:10.919
Our main outcomes that we want from this is that you will understand three key things.

20
00:02:10.919 --> 00:02:22.430
The parameters that you can control on a material, how you can change these parameters and the type of different effects that you can create.

21
00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:29.430
So we will be going through a lot of different examples that will show you all of the different ways that we'll be able to do this.

22
00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:36.740
So let's start off talking about what you can actually control.

23
00:02:36.740 --> 00:02:41.840
Well, within a material, we can control pretty much anything.

24
00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:45.840
We can pass in variables and we can change stuff for runtime.

25
00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:50.840
It's going to allow us to control most different settings of the material.

26
00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:54.840
So to start with, we'll be able to control all the standard nodes.

27
00:02:54.840 --> 00:03:00.840
So standard nodes would be the base colour, normal, roughness, emissive, metallic,

28
00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:08.840
specular, any of the textures that we're using and the opacity or the opacity mask.

29
00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:16.840
So all of these are manipulatable at runtime and we'll be able to connect it to gameplay functionality.

30
00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:21.939
One of the next things that's important to understand is something called UVs.

31
00:03:21.939 --> 00:03:26.939
Now, you're probably aware of what UVs do and how they are used.

32
00:03:26.939 --> 00:03:34.939
Now, they're standardly used as a means of wrapping textures around meshes.

33
00:03:34.939 --> 00:03:50.939
Now, a UV space will usually be a UV map, which will tell us where a pixel from a texture would need to be mapped upon a pixel from a mesh.

34
00:03:50.939 --> 00:03:58.939
So if we were to unwrap a texture, how would we wrap it around the object that we've created in 3D?

35
00:03:58.939 --> 00:04:03.030
Now, we're going to be using UVs in lots of different ways.

36
00:04:03.030 --> 00:04:11.030
Now, the model UVs, these ones here, this is the standard use of UVs you're probably used to.

37
00:04:11.030 --> 00:04:18.579
And this is where the texture is just mapped upon a static mesh.

38
00:04:18.579 --> 00:04:22.579
But next up, what we have here is screen space UVs.

39
00:04:22.579 --> 00:04:38.579
So what this is doing is rather than using the UVs for the object, it is actually taking the UVs from the screen and using that to figure out how to show you the texture.

40
00:04:38.579 --> 00:04:44.579
So you can see here as I move around, the texture is mapping to the screen rather than to the object.

41
00:04:44.579 --> 00:04:56.579
Next up is something called world space. Rather than using the local object, what we're doing is we're looking at where a specific pixel appears within the entire level.

42
00:04:56.579 --> 00:05:01.579
So the world. And we're using that in order to map texture.

43
00:05:01.579 --> 00:05:12.670
So you can see here, for example, that if I was to move this cube around, the texture would not move with it.

44
00:05:12.670 --> 00:05:21.800
That's because it's tracking it in the world space. Now, we work in three planes, X, Y and Z.

45
00:05:21.800 --> 00:05:30.800
So you can see there is some oddity in this when it comes to a specific angle because it's trying to map it in those three planes.

46
00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:37.930
And if you are halfway between two of them, it's not going to easily fit within those parameters.

47
00:05:37.930 --> 00:05:53.019
Finally, what we have is the object bounds. Now, this looks a little bit like our surface space, but all of the objects that we have within Unreal have something that's called a bounds.

48
00:05:53.019 --> 00:06:00.019
And a bounds is a invisible box, which is often used for something called frustrum culling.

49
00:06:00.019 --> 00:06:07.019
It is used for determining if a mesh should be rendered to the screen.

50
00:06:07.019 --> 00:06:12.019
So it's looking to see if it is within the frustrum of the camera.

51
00:06:12.019 --> 00:06:17.019
It is its main use. However, we can also use it for lots of other different things.

52
00:06:17.019 --> 00:06:29.019
In this case, we are using it to map something to an object over its bounds and allow us to create effects that would work on numerous different meshes,

53
00:06:29.019 --> 00:06:37.019
because it uses the bounds rather than the mesh itself as a method of mapping textures to it.

54
00:06:37.019 --> 00:06:44.699
So UVs are an important element to understand. So how can we manipulate UVs?

55
00:06:44.699 --> 00:06:49.699
Well, we can do them in a number of ways, but the standard methods would be to pan the UVs.

56
00:06:49.699 --> 00:07:00.889
This is going to allow us to move the UVs in a specific direction, often used for creating things like water running down a material.

57
00:07:00.889 --> 00:07:14.449
We can rotate them in order to create different effects, or we can scale. Next up, something that we haven't talked about yet, and that is World Position Offset.

58
00:07:14.449 --> 00:07:21.769
Now, what World Position Offset does is it can be found. Let me just open up one of these materials.

59
00:07:21.769 --> 00:07:29.769
So we can find it here on the output pen for our material, World Position Offset.

60
00:07:29.769 --> 00:07:41.769
And what this is, is it is determining how we move a pixel, or in this case, a vertices.

61
00:07:41.769 --> 00:07:52.769
So we can manipulate the vertices which are within a mesh, and we can use World Position Offset to animate it or to move those.

62
00:07:52.769 --> 00:08:09.769
Now, this is most commonly used in foliage tools, because what you can do in that case is you can create a relatively cheap swaying of trees or grass.

63
00:08:10.769 --> 00:08:14.769
So that it's a cheap method to create some kind of effect like that.

64
00:08:14.769 --> 00:08:25.990
Now, the main thing to note is if I was to jump in to wireframe mode and select, you can see that it's moving these vertices around.

65
00:08:25.990 --> 00:08:30.990
So that is what is being offset by World Position Offset.

66
00:08:30.990 --> 00:08:38.990
Now, the vertices are not actually moving. They're only being rendered. They're only being moving in the rendering.

67
00:08:38.990 --> 00:08:43.990
So this isn't going to change any collisions or anything like that.

68
00:08:43.990 --> 00:08:51.120
Now, in this, as you can see, I've noted here that we're using the bounding box as a mask.

69
00:08:51.120 --> 00:08:57.120
And you can see at the bottom, we have a rather dark feet and a rather light top.

70
00:08:57.120 --> 00:09:00.120
That's the first use of using the bounding box.

71
00:09:00.120 --> 00:09:10.120
So all of these different things are swaying, but the effect is becoming less the further down money we get in this situation.

72
00:09:10.120 --> 00:09:23.120
And even though we have multiple material slots for these characters, because they're both working off of the same bounding box, it functions sort of seamlessly.

73
00:09:23.309 --> 00:09:33.309
So we can see here, if you want to check them out, we've got different material suggestions for each of the shrink and grow.

74
00:09:33.309 --> 00:09:42.820
So let's open one of these up. If I open up Rotate, you're going to see that we've got a couple of different parts here that are worth looking at.

75
00:09:42.820 --> 00:09:49.139
Firstly, we are calculating the bounding box mask using Z.

76
00:09:49.139 --> 00:09:54.139
And you can see that this is the calculation on how to do that.

77
00:09:54.139 --> 00:10:00.139
And I'm displaying this part of the base color so we can see the change.

78
00:10:00.139 --> 00:10:06.590
And I've just got the rotate around the world axis here.

79
00:10:06.590 --> 00:10:11.980
And I'm just animating using time through a sine wave.

80
00:10:11.980 --> 00:10:14.980
Multipliers are just going to determinate the speed.

81
00:10:14.980 --> 00:10:21.100
OK. And all of them look roughly like that. So feel free to check out any of the other materials.

82
00:10:21.100 --> 00:10:27.100
So just as an end point to this little bit of theory, let's talk over what we're going to be covering, really.

83
00:10:27.100 --> 00:10:37.100
So we have our materials and our materials are what we physically see, which is rendered within the scene.

84
00:10:37.100 --> 00:10:43.100
And we can effect or change values in these from numerous different places.

85
00:10:43.100 --> 00:10:49.100
So the most commonplace probably going to use is something that's called a dynamic material instance.

86
00:10:49.100 --> 00:11:00.100
And what these are is we will create a material instance at runtime and assign it to a mesh or a decal or something else.

87
00:11:00.100 --> 00:11:04.100
And we can then change those variables.

88
00:11:04.100 --> 00:11:10.100
So we can change them like they're variables and we can update the material within our coding.

89
00:11:10.100 --> 00:11:18.100
So within Blueprints or C++. So that way we have a programmatic control over what the material does.

90
00:11:18.100 --> 00:11:30.100
Next thing we have is the mesh itself. So we can save variables to the mesh itself and we can change some of their settings.

91
00:11:30.100 --> 00:11:38.100
Then we can draw on those settings within a material and we can update and we can change stuff by just looking at the mesh.

92
00:11:38.100 --> 00:11:42.870
Also, we have something that's called a material parameter collection.

93
00:11:42.870 --> 00:11:53.870
What a material parameter collection is, is it is an asset where we can save a almost like universal file where we can save variables in it.

94
00:11:53.870 --> 00:12:03.870
And these variables can be accessed directly within a material without having to create an instance, without having to do any programing.

95
00:12:03.870 --> 00:12:08.870
And it can update any material which is using those parameters.

96
00:12:08.870 --> 00:12:20.899
Finally, there are a number of variables which are accessible in the material itself, which we can use in order to make it seem dynamic, such as time.

97
00:12:20.899 --> 00:12:30.899
Time is something that we often run through a sine wave in order to animate, like when we have those back and forth animations with the world position offset.

98
00:12:30.899 --> 00:12:39.899
You can also use screen location or things such as camera distance in order to change the animations.

99
00:12:39.899 --> 00:12:49.029
So that will bring us to the end of this first video, where we're just going over a bunch of the theory and making sure everyone has the foundations in place

100
00:12:49.029 --> 00:12:53.029
so that we can jump into the examples and exercises.

101
00:12:53.029 --> 00:13:03.309
OK, so we've looked at what you can affect within a material and also where you can update information.

102
00:13:03.309 --> 00:13:10.889
Let's look at the different ways that materials can be displayed within Unreal.

103
00:13:10.889 --> 00:13:16.889
So the first way that you're probably very accustomed to is surface.

104
00:13:16.889 --> 00:13:30.940
So what we're doing in this instance is we're taking a skeletal mesh or a static mesh and we are wrapping the material around it so that it can be displayed within the world.

105
00:13:30.940 --> 00:13:34.940
Next up, what we have is a decal.

106
00:13:34.940 --> 00:13:48.940
And a decal works very similar to surface, but rather than it being part of the mesh when it's rendered, it is projected onto a mesh afterwards.

107
00:13:48.940 --> 00:13:55.940
So you can see here as I move this around, it will move around the different elements.

108
00:13:55.940 --> 00:14:00.940
And if I was to rotate it, you can see how the projection is stretched.

109
00:14:00.940 --> 00:14:11.940
This is great for things such as road markings or grime and muck or cracks or something like that.

110
00:14:11.940 --> 00:14:16.940
Something that you want to put over a material to make it look a little bit different.

111
00:14:16.940 --> 00:14:20.460
Next up, we have light functions.

112
00:14:20.460 --> 00:14:28.460
Now, light functions, you'll find a slot for the material in the details panel of most light actors.

113
00:14:28.460 --> 00:14:31.460
And what these do is they work as a mask.

114
00:14:31.460 --> 00:14:41.460
So rather than actually projecting the material, it's going to prevent it showing through.

115
00:14:41.460 --> 00:14:47.460
So you can see here that the black of the face no longer this spotlight shows up on.

116
00:14:47.460 --> 00:14:54.159
Now, these are mostly used in order to get a more realistic looking light.

117
00:14:54.159 --> 00:15:04.159
So when you've got a standard bulb that is being projected against the wall, it won't be just a smooth transition.

118
00:15:04.159 --> 00:15:11.159
It will actually show some kind of pattern depending on what kind of bulb you're using.

119
00:15:11.159 --> 00:15:18.159
So if you want to get a very realistic looking bulb, that's where you'd use a relatively cheap light function.

120
00:15:18.159 --> 00:15:20.159
Next up, we have widgets.

121
00:15:20.159 --> 00:15:24.159
Now, widgets are Unreal's heads up display.

122
00:15:24.159 --> 00:15:31.159
A widget uses Unreal's system that's called UMG, which stands for Unreal Motion Graphics.

123
00:15:31.159 --> 00:15:35.159
And this is when you want to create something 2D.

124
00:15:35.159 --> 00:15:45.159
Now, most commonly, this would be things such as menu systems or heads up displays that you want to attach to the screen.

125
00:15:45.159 --> 00:15:48.159
However, they can actually be placed within the world.

126
00:15:48.159 --> 00:15:55.159
So you can see here that we have this example and this is our widget.

127
00:15:55.159 --> 00:16:03.159
And it's actually rather than it being on the screen itself, we are placing a plane within the world

128
00:16:03.159 --> 00:16:07.159
and we are rendering that widget onto the plane.

129
00:16:07.159 --> 00:16:20.159
This is often used for things such as like health bars on characters or any menus that you're using within virtual reality.

130
00:16:20.159 --> 00:16:27.159
Because in virtual reality, you really don't want to be doing stuff on the screen itself because it really messes with the eyes.

131
00:16:27.159 --> 00:16:31.929
So that's widgets in world space.

132
00:16:31.929 --> 00:16:35.929
The other option we've got is in screen space and I'll just show you this.

133
00:16:35.929 --> 00:16:41.929
So in screen space, you can see we've got our little character's face that appears in front of us.

134
00:16:41.929 --> 00:16:46.929
But it appears on the actual screen rather than in the world.

135
00:16:46.929 --> 00:16:53.279
And this would be your menu systems and your heads up display, health bar and so on.

136
00:16:53.279 --> 00:16:59.470
OK, final one I want to talk about is the post process material.

137
00:16:59.470 --> 00:17:07.470
So if you are working with Unreal, the way Unreal will work is it will go away

138
00:17:07.470 --> 00:17:14.470
and it's going to look at each of the different material instances that are on the screen.

139
00:17:14.470 --> 00:17:19.470
And it's going to put them into little buckets to calculate what they'll look like.

140
00:17:19.470 --> 00:17:25.470
And then afterwards, it's going to composite them all together into the G buffer.

141
00:17:25.470 --> 00:17:32.470
So we'd get elements like we would get a base colour pass, lighting pass, metallic roughness and so on.

142
00:17:32.470 --> 00:17:37.470
And these are all composited together in order to make the final output.

143
00:17:37.470 --> 00:17:42.470
And that final output, we can then do something with it afterwards.

144
00:17:42.470 --> 00:17:48.470
So after the scene has been rendered, we can apply a material to it.

145
00:17:48.470 --> 00:17:54.470
In this case, I've just used it to carve a section out from the centre of the screen

146
00:17:54.470 --> 00:17:59.470
and show us the green face. But it can be used for lots and lots of different things.

147
00:17:59.470 --> 00:18:09.470
And the great thing is you have access to all of the G buffers that have been created previously to it.

148
00:18:09.470 --> 00:18:16.470
So usually these are using something that's called post process volumes,

149
00:18:16.470 --> 00:18:21.470
which can be set to Unbound or just a specific one.

150
00:18:21.470 --> 00:18:25.470
As you can see, this appears when I enter this box.

151
00:18:25.470 --> 00:18:27.630
So that's post process.

152
00:18:27.630 --> 00:18:33.660
So we've gone over all of the different things that we can change within a material.

153
00:18:33.660 --> 00:18:36.660
We've looked at where we might get the inputs in order to control them.

154
00:18:36.660 --> 00:18:42.859
And then we've looked at the different methods of display for materials.

155
00:18:42.859 --> 00:18:44.859
That's it for the theory.

156
00:18:44.859 --> 00:18:48.859
Next, we're going to start looking at some specific examples

157
00:18:48.859 --> 00:18:53.859
and do a few exercises to see how we would control them.

158
00:18:53.859 --> 00:18:55.109
Thank you very much.
